//  status "unsure" (never drawn by the Question of the Day or the mocks):
//   - u3-h-doe-defendant: Rule 15(c)(1)(C) and a "John Doe" defendant. The stem now isolates the "mistake" prong (the officer learned of the
//     suit within the Rule 4(m) period; no law governing the limitations period allows relation back). The majority reading (ignorance of
//     the defendant's identity is not a "mistake concerning the proper party's identity") is secondary-source law; check the circuits and
//     Krupski's treatment before shipping.
//  Deep verification pass (2026-11-02): lesson 1 split into "Pleading a claim and answering" and "Amendment and relation back", and the
//  motions lesson split into Rule 12 and summary judgment/JMOL, so lesson ids from u3l2 on are renumbered (u3l1-u3l14); stored progress
//  keyed by the old ids shifts. New: u3-r-collateral-order (Cohen's three requirements), Lucky Brand (2020) on post-complaint conduct,
//  Rule 26(b)(5)(A) privilege-log and 26(c) protective-order hypos, Rule 45 100-mile, Rule 42(a), Rule 12(e) and Rule 50(c) hypos, and
//  u3-c-blom-bank (BLOM Bank v. Honickman, No. 23-1259, June 5, 2025; syllabus read on Cornell LII; recent). u3-h-cafa-minimal removed:
//  CAFA is taught in u2 (u2-h-cafa, u2-n-cafa), and Owen Equipment v. Kroger lives there too.
//  Read at Cornell LII (full opinion text, 2026-11-02), so now status "general": u3-c-coney-island (No. 24-808, Jan. 20, 2026: the
//  "reasonable time" requirement of Rule 60(c)(1) applies to a Rule 60(b)(4) motion; the Court did not say what is reasonable) and
//  u3-c-geo-group (No. 24-758, Feb. 25, 2026: Yearsley is a merits defense, not an immunity from suit, so its denial is not a collateral
//  order). Still worth reading the slip opinions on supremecourt.gov before upgrading either to "book".
//  Secondary-source (status "general") points worth a second look: Unitherm and Dimick/Hetzel formulations; the Parklane factors; the
//  Semtek formulation; Temple v. Synthes; Edmonson; Taylor v. Sturgell and the Restatement sections on acquittals; Dupree and Ortiz; the
//  Rule 16(b)(4) "good cause first" majority view; and the Rule 51 timing language (the current text speaks of the opportunity to object
//  before the instructions and arguments are delivered, or "promptly after learning", not "before the jury retires").
//  Currency: the December 1, 2025 amendments to Rules 16(b)(3)(B)(iv) and 26(f)(3)(D) (privilege-log timing and method) are reflected. No
//  civil-rule amendment is known to take effect on December 1, 2026; proposals published in August 2025 (for example to Rules 7.1, 26, 41,
//  45 and 81) could not take effect before December 1, 2027, so they do not touch the February or July 2027 sittings (confirm on
//  uscourts.gov before shipping).
//  NextGen tags: Rule 23, jury selection and instructions, Rules 16(b)(4)/16(e), 41, 49, 52, 59, 60 and 68 items are tagged ["mbe"]; the
//  NextGen outline keeps Rule 12(c), summary judgment, JMOL, default, preclusion elements and the Rule 26(f) conference.
