            stem: "A driver sued another driver in federal court for damage to her car from a collision and won a final judgment. The next year she sued the same defendant in federal court for her personal injuries from the same collision. Both actions are governed by the transactional approach to claim preclusion. The defendant moved to dismiss the second action.",
            call: "How should the court rule on the motion?",
            options: [
              { text: "Grant, because both claims arise from the same transaction and the first judgment was a final judgment on the merits between the same parties.", why: "Rest. (2d) Judgments §24: one transaction gives rise to one claim, whatever the theory or remedy." },
              { text: "Deny, because property damage and personal injury are different claims.", why: "That is the older rule, which the transactional approach rejects.", trap: 11 },
              { text: "Deny, because the second action seeks different damages.", why: "A different remedy does not create a different claim.", trap: 5 },
              { text: "Grant, but only if the first judgment followed a jury trial.", why: "Any final judgment on the merits has claim-preclusive effect.", trap: 9 },
            ],
            answer: 0,
            facts: ["her personal injuries from the same collision"],
            say: "One collision is one claim: win the car damage, lose the whiplash action.",
